Halo Mode: When the switch is in the "down" position, the device creates a protective zone that Roomba will not enter. This prevents vacuum from bumping into items you want to protect (e.g. a dog bowl or vase) or crossing into undesired areas (e.g. a corner or under a desk). The halo is invisible and reaches approximately 24 inches from the center of the device. (See Figure 3)


<PIC:Manual32_5>
{{image description: Figure 3 showing a robot vacuum near a pet bowl and a Virtual Wall barrier; green arrows indicate the protected area and a 4 ft / 1.2 m distance.}}

Virtual Wall Mode: When the switch is in the "up" position, the device functions as a virtual wall. This means that you can set it to block openings of up to 10 feet. It creates an invisible, cone-shaped barrier that only vacuum can see. (See Figure 2)


<PIC:Manual32_6>
{{image description: Figure 2 showing a Virtual Wall barrier along a wall with a green blocked zone and arrow; measurement label reads 10 ft / 3 m.}}

1. Press bin release button to remove bin. (See Figure 4)
<PIC:Manual32_8>
{{image description: Figure 4 showing the dust bin removed from the front of the robot vacuum, with the robot body behind it.}}

2. Open bin door to empty bin. (See Figure 5)
<PIC:Manual32_9>
{{image description: Figure 5 showing a hand emptying the robot vacuum dust bin into a trash can with the bin door open.}}

3. Remove filter by grasping the yellow tab. (See Figure 6)
<PIC:Manual32_10>
{{image description: Figure 6 showing the dust bin opened with the filter visible in an inset; the green filter is partly pulled out.}}

4. Shake off debris by tapping the filter against your trash container. (See Figure 7)
<PIC:Manual32_11>
{{image description: Figure 7 showing a hand rinsing or cleaning a rectangular filter over a sink or container.}}

1. Remove and empty the bin. (See Figure 8)
<PIC:Manual32_12>
{{image description: Figure 8 showing the dust bin removed from the robot vacuum before reinsertion.}}

2. Wipe the sensors with a clean, dry cloth. (See Figure 9)
<PIC:Manual32_13>
{{image description: Figure 9 underside view of the robot vacuum with two circular inset views highlighting internal bin or sensor areas.}}

3. Wipe the inner and outer sensor ports on the bin with a clean, dry cloth. (See Figure 10)
<PIC:Manual32_14>
{{image description: Figure 10 showing the dust bin with labeled Inner Sensor Ports and Outer Sensor Ports.}}

1. Pull firmly on the front wheel to remove it. (See Figure 11)
<PIC:Manual32_15>
{{image description: Figure 11 showing a front caster wheel assembly with labels Wheel, Axle, and Housing, and green arrows indicating removal direction.}}

1. Use a coin or small screwdriver to remove the screw. (See Figure 12)
<PIC:Manual32_16>
{{image description: Figure 12 showing a hand using a small tool or screwdriver near the underside of the robot vacuum.}}

1. Wipe the sensor with a clean, dry cloth. Do not spray cleaning solution directly onto the sensors or sensor openings. (See Figure 13)
<PIC:Manual32_17>
{{image description: Figure 13 underside view of the robot vacuum with the Floor Tracking Sensor labeled near the edge.}}

2. Wipe the charging contacts on Roomba and the Home Base with a clean, dry cloth. (See Figure 14)
<PIC:Manual32_18>
{{image description: Figure 14 showing charging contacts on the underside of the robot vacuum and matching charging contacts on the dock.}}

1. Pinch the yellow extractor frame release tabs, lift up the extractor frame and remove any obstructions. (See Figure 15)
<PIC:Manual32_19>
{{image description: Figure 15 showing the robot vacuum underside with the green extractor frame lifted open above the debris extractors.}}

2. Remove the extractors and remove the yellow extractor caps. Remove any hair or debris that has collected underneath the caps and around the metal axles. Reinstall the extractor caps. (See Figure 16)
<PIC:Manual32_20>
{{image description: Figure 16 showing two debris extractors removed from the robot, with green extractor caps separated from the ends.}}

3. Remove hair and debris from the square and hexagonal plastic pegs on the other side of the extractors. (See Figure 17)
<PIC:Manual32_21>
{{image description: Figure 17 showing a hand removing or cleaning debris extractor caps from the ends of the extractors.}}

4. Clear the vacuum path. (See Figure 18)
<PIC:Manual32_22>
{{image description: Figure 18 underside view of the robot vacuum with two large green arrows indicating the direction to reinstall the extractors or close the extractor frame.}}
